Let $f_1$, $f_2$, and $f_3$ be the components in the given order. Differentiate each scalar component separately.

For component 1, use the power and chain rules:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_{1}'(t)&=\frac{d}{dt}\left(- 3 t^{2}\right) \\ &=- 6 t
\end{aligned}
$$

For component 2, use the trigonometric and chain rules:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_{2}'(t)&=\frac{d}{dt}\left(2 \cos{\left(t \right)}\right) \\ &=2\cdot 1\cdot - \sin{\left(t \right)} \\ &=- 2 \sin{\left(t \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

For component 3, use the square-root and chain rules:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_{3}'(t)&=\frac{d}{dt}\left(\sqrt{t^{2} + 1}\right) \\ &=\frac{t}{\sqrt{t^{2} + 1}}
\end{aligned}
$$

Thus

$$
\mathbf{f}'(t)=\begin{bmatrix}- 6 t \\ - 2 \sin{\left(t \right)} \\ \frac{t}{\sqrt{t^{2} + 1}}\end{bmatrix}.
$$

Substitute $t=\pi$ into the derivative, after differentiating:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_{1}'(\pi)&=- 6 \left(\pi\right) \\ &=- 6 \pi \\ f_{2}'(\pi)&=- 2 \sin{\left(\left(\pi\right) \right)} \\ &=0 \\ f_{3}'(\pi)&=\frac{\left(\pi\right)}{\sqrt{\left(\pi\right)^{2} + 1}} \\ &=\frac{\pi}{\sqrt{1 + \pi^{2}}}
\end{aligned}
$$

Keeping the component order gives

$$
\mathbf{f}'(\pi)=\begin{bmatrix}- 6 \pi \\ 0 \\ \frac{\pi}{\sqrt{1 + \pi^{2}}}\end{bmatrix}.
$$

Let $f_1$, $f_2$, and $f_3$ be the components in the given order. Differentiate each scalar component separately.

For component 1, use the square-root and chain rules:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_{1}'(t)&=\frac{d}{dt}\left(\sqrt{t^{2} + 1}\right) \\ &=\frac{t}{\sqrt{t^{2} + 1}}
\end{aligned}
$$

For component 2, use the power and chain rules:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_{2}'(t)&=\frac{d}{dt}\left(\left(t + 2\right)^{2}\right) \\ &=2 t + 4
\end{aligned}
$$

For component 3, use the trigonometric and chain rules:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_{3}'(t)&=\frac{d}{dt}\left(2 \cos{\left(2 t \right)}\right) \\ &=2\cdot 2\cdot - \sin{\left(2 t \right)} \\ &=- 4 \sin{\left(2 t \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

Thus

$$
\mathbf{f}'(t)=\begin{bmatrix}\frac{t}{\sqrt{t^{2} + 1}} \\ 2 t + 4 \\ - 4 \sin{\left(2 t \right)}\end{bmatrix}.
$$

Substitute $t=\frac{\pi}{2}$ into the derivative, after differentiating:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_{1}'\left(\frac{\pi}{2}\right)&=\frac{\left(\frac{\pi}{2}\right)}{\sqrt{\left(\frac{\pi}{2}\right)^{2} + 1}} \\ &=\frac{\pi}{2 \sqrt{1 + \frac{\pi^{2}}{4}}} \\ f_{2}'\left(\frac{\pi}{2}\right)&=2 \left(\frac{\pi}{2}\right) + 4 \\ &=\pi + 4 \\ f_{3}'\left(\frac{\pi}{2}\right)&=- 4 \sin{\left(2 \left(\frac{\pi}{2}\right) \right)} \\ &=0
\end{aligned}
$$

Keeping the component order gives

$$
\mathbf{f}'\left(\frac{\pi}{2}\right)=\begin{bmatrix}\frac{\pi}{2 \sqrt{1 + \frac{\pi^{2}}{4}}} \\ \pi + 4 \\ 0\end{bmatrix}.
$$

Let $f_1$, $f_2$, and $f_3$ be the components in the given order. Differentiate each scalar component separately.

For component 1, use the power and sum rules:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_{1}'(t)&=\frac{d}{dt}\left(2 t^{2}\right) \\ &=4 t
\end{aligned}
$$

For component 2, use the trigonometric, sum and power rules:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_{2}'(t)&=\frac{d}{dt}\left(2 t - \sin{\left(t \right)}\right) \\ &=2 - \cos{\left(t \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

For component 3, use the trigonometric and chain rules:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_{3}'(t)&=\frac{d}{dt}\left(- \cos{\left(3 t \right)}\right) \\ &=-1\cdot 3\cdot - \sin{\left(3 t \right)} \\ &=3 \sin{\left(3 t \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

Thus

$$
\mathbf{f}'(t)=\begin{bmatrix}4 t \\ 2 - \cos{\left(t \right)} \\ 3 \sin{\left(3 t \right)}\end{bmatrix}.
$$

Substitute $t=\pi$ into the derivative, after differentiating:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_{1}'(\pi)&=4 \left(\pi\right) \\ &=4 \pi \\ f_{2}'(\pi)&=2 - \cos{\left(\left(\pi\right) \right)} \\ &=3 \\ f_{3}'(\pi)&=3 \sin{\left(3 \left(\pi\right) \right)} \\ &=0
\end{aligned}
$$

Keeping the component order gives

$$
\mathbf{f}'(\pi)=\begin{bmatrix}4 \pi \\ 3 \\ 0\end{bmatrix}.
$$

Let $f_1$, $f_2$, and $f_3$ be the components in the given order. Differentiate each scalar component separately.

For component 1, use the negative-power and chain rules:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_{1}'(t)&=\frac{d}{dt}\left(\frac{1}{t}\right) \\ &=- \frac{1}{t^{2}}
\end{aligned}
$$

For component 2, use the power and sum rules:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_{2}'(t)&=\frac{d}{dt}\left(- 3 t^{2}\right) \\ &=- 6 t
\end{aligned}
$$

For component 3, use the product rule:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_{3}'(t)&=\frac{d}{dt}\left(- t e^{t}\right) \\ &=-1\left(1\cdot e^t+t\cdot e^t\right) \\ &=- t e^{t} - e^{t}
\end{aligned}
$$

Thus

$$
\mathbf{f}'(t)=\begin{bmatrix}- \frac{1}{t^{2}} \\ - 6 t \\ - t e^{t} - e^{t}\end{bmatrix}.
$$

Substitute $t=2$ into the derivative, after differentiating:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_{1}'(2)&=- \frac{1}{\left(2\right)^{2}} \\ &=- \frac{1}{4} \\ f_{2}'(2)&=- 6 \left(2\right) \\ &=-12 \\ f_{3}'(2)&=- \left(2\right) e^{\left(2\right)} - e^{\left(2\right)} \\ &=- 3 e^{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

Keeping the component order gives

$$
\mathbf{f}'(2)=\begin{bmatrix}- \frac{1}{4} \\ -12 \\ - 3 e^{2}\end{bmatrix}.
$$

Let $f_1$, $f_2$, and $f_3$ be the components in the given order. Differentiate each scalar component separately.

For component 1, use the inverse-tangent and chain rules:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_{1}'(t)&=\frac{d}{dt}\left(\arctan{\left(3 t \right)}\right) \\ &=1\cdot\frac{3}{1+(3 t)^2} \\ &=\frac{3}{9 t^{2} + 1}
\end{aligned}
$$

For component 2, use the negative-power and chain rules:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_{2}'(t)&=\frac{d}{dt}\left(\frac{3}{t + 1}\right) \\ &=- \frac{3}{\left(t + 1\right)^{2}}
\end{aligned}
$$

For component 3, use the power and sum rules:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_{3}'(t)&=\frac{d}{dt}\left(- 3 t^{2} + 3 t\right) \\ &=3 - 6 t
\end{aligned}
$$

Thus

$$
\mathbf{f}'(t)=\begin{bmatrix}\frac{3}{9 t^{2} + 1} \\ - \frac{3}{\left(t + 1\right)^{2}} \\ 3 - 6 t\end{bmatrix}.
$$

Substitute $t=0$ into the derivative, after differentiating:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_{1}'(0)&=\frac{3}{9 \left(0\right)^{2} + 1} \\ &=3 \\ f_{2}'(0)&=- \frac{3}{\left(\left(0\right) + 1\right)^{2}} \\ &=-3 \\ f_{3}'(0)&=3 - 6 \left(0\right) \\ &=3
\end{aligned}
$$

Keeping the component order gives

$$
\mathbf{f}'(0)=\begin{bmatrix}3 \\ -3 \\ 3\end{bmatrix}.
$$

Let $f_1$, $f_2$, and $f_3$ be the components in the given order. Differentiate each scalar component separately.

For component 1, use the product rule with logarithm chain rule:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_{1}'(t)&=\frac{d}{dt}\left(t \ln{\left(2 t + 1 \right)}\right) \\ &=1\left(1\cdot \ln{\left(2 t + 1 \right)}+t\cdot\frac{2}{2 t + 1}\right) \\ &=\frac{2 t}{2 t + 1} + \ln{\left(2 t + 1 \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

For component 2, use the power and sum rules:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_{2}'(t)&=\frac{d}{dt}\left(3 t^{2}\right) \\ &=6 t
\end{aligned}
$$

For component 3, use the sum and product rules; cancel opposite sine terms:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_{3}'(t)&=\frac{d}{dt}\left(2 t \sin{\left(t \right)} + 2 \cos{\left(t \right)}\right) \\ &=2\left(-\sin t+\sin t+t\cos t\right) \\ &=2 t \cos{\left(t \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

Thus

$$
\mathbf{f}'(t)=\begin{bmatrix}\frac{2 t}{2 t + 1} + \ln{\left(2 t + 1 \right)} \\ 6 t \\ 2 t \cos{\left(t \right)}\end{bmatrix}.
$$

Substitute $t=1$ into the derivative, after differentiating:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_{1}'(1)&=\frac{2 \left(1\right)}{2 \left(1\right) + 1} + \ln{\left(2 \left(1\right) + 1 \right)} \\ &=\frac{2}{3} + \ln{\left(3 \right)} \\ f_{2}'(1)&=6 \left(1\right) \\ &=6 \\ f_{3}'(1)&=2 \left(1\right) \cos{\left(\left(1\right) \right)} \\ &=2 \cos{\left(1 \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

Keeping the component order gives

$$
\mathbf{f}'(1)=\begin{bmatrix}\frac{2}{3} + \ln{\left(3 \right)} \\ 6 \\ 2 \cos{\left(1 \right)}\end{bmatrix}.
$$

