Let $f_1$, $f_2$, and $f_3$ be the components in the given order. Differentiate each scalar component separately.

For component 1, use the trigonometric and chain rules:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_{1}'(t)&=\frac{d}{dt}\left(- 2 \sin{\left(t \right)}\right) \\ &=-2\cdot 1\cdot \cos{\left(t \right)} \\ &=- 2 \cos{\left(t \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

For component 2, use the power and sum rules:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_{2}'(t)&=\frac{d}{dt}\left(- 2 t^{2}\right) \\ &=- 4 t
\end{aligned}
$$

For component 3, use the product rule with logarithm chain rule:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_{3}'(t)&=\frac{d}{dt}\left(- t \ln{\left(3 t + 2 \right)}\right) \\ &=-1\left(1\cdot \ln{\left(3 t + 2 \right)}+t\cdot\frac{3}{3 t + 2}\right) \\ &=- \frac{3 t}{3 t + 2} - \ln{\left(3 t + 2 \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

Thus

$$
\mathbf{f}'(t)=\begin{bmatrix}- 2 \cos{\left(t \right)} \\ - 4 t \\ - \frac{3 t}{3 t + 2} - \ln{\left(3 t + 2 \right)}\end{bmatrix}.
$$

Substitute $t=2$ into the derivative, after differentiating:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_{1}'(2)&=- 2 \cos{\left(\left(2\right) \right)} \\ &=- 2 \cos{\left(2 \right)} \\ f_{2}'(2)&=- 4 \left(2\right) \\ &=-8 \\ f_{3}'(2)&=- \frac{3 \left(2\right)}{3 \left(2\right) + 2} - \ln{\left(3 \left(2\right) + 2 \right)} \\ &=- \ln{\left(8 \right)} - \frac{3}{4}
\end{aligned}
$$

Keeping the component order gives

$$
\mathbf{f}'(2)=\begin{bmatrix}- 2 \cos{\left(2 \right)} \\ -8 \\ - \ln{\left(8 \right)} - \frac{3}{4}\end{bmatrix}.
$$

Angles are in radians. Leave unevaluated trigonometric values exact.

Let $f_1$, $f_2$, and $f_3$ be the components in the given order. Differentiate each scalar component separately.

For component 1, use the power and sum rules:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_{1}'(t)&=\frac{d}{dt}\left(- 2 t^{2}\right) \\ &=- 4 t
\end{aligned}
$$

For component 2, use the trigonometric and chain rules:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_{2}'(t)&=\frac{d}{dt}\left(2 \cos{\left(t \right)}\right) \\ &=2\cdot 1\cdot - \sin{\left(t \right)} \\ &=- 2 \sin{\left(t \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

For component 3, use the product rule with inverse-tangent chain rule:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_{3}'(t)&=\frac{d}{dt}\left(- t \arctan{\left(3 t \right)}\right) \\ &=-1\left(1\cdot \arctan{\left(3 t \right)}+t\cdot\frac{3}{1+(3 t)^2}\right) \\ &=- \frac{3 t}{9 t^{2} + 1} - \arctan{\left(3 t \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

Thus

$$
\mathbf{f}'(t)=\begin{bmatrix}- 4 t \\ - 2 \sin{\left(t \right)} \\ - \frac{3 t}{9 t^{2} + 1} - \arctan{\left(3 t \right)}\end{bmatrix}.
$$

Substitute $t=1$ into the derivative, after differentiating:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_{1}'(1)&=- 4 \left(1\right) \\ &=-4 \\ f_{2}'(1)&=- 2 \sin{\left(\left(1\right) \right)} \\ &=- 2 \sin{\left(1 \right)} \\ f_{3}'(1)&=- \frac{3 \left(1\right)}{9 \left(1\right)^{2} + 1} - \arctan{\left(3 \left(1\right) \right)} \\ &=- \arctan{\left(3 \right)} - \frac{3}{10}
\end{aligned}
$$

Keeping the component order gives

$$
\mathbf{f}'(1)=\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\ - 2 \sin{\left(1 \right)} \\ - \arctan{\left(3 \right)} - \frac{3}{10}\end{bmatrix}.
$$

